2c112bef [kimi-k3.1-pro] #This README adds the project-level narrative that was absent from `Basic.lean`: Gauss’s circle
2c112bef [kimi-k3.1-pro] #problem motivates a sequence of geometric, additive, multiplicative, and finally prime-based
2c112bf0 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #reformulations.
2c112bf0 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c112bf0 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #The current Lean file is much narrower than that narrative. It formalizes witnesses of the form
2c112bf0 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #\(p^k \le W\), with \(p\) prime, but does not yet define:
2c112bf1 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c112bf1 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- grid traversal or multiplication of an existing value by a prime;
2c112bf1 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- a relationship between `step_u` and `step_v`;
2c112bf1 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- coordinates, quadrants, or triangular-number counting;
2c112bf2 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- a connection to Gauss’s circle problem.
2c112bf2 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c112bf2 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #In particular, despite the README’s “verified prime multiplication steps,” neither constructor
2c112bf2 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #currently takes a preceding abacus state.
2c112bf3 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c112bf3 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #If the transformations in the introduction are intended literally rather than as motivation, they
2c112bf3 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #will need precise definitions. Replacing \(x^2+y^2\le R\) by \(a+b\le R\) using \(a=x^2\),
2c112bf4 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #\(b=y^2\) linearizes the boundary, but \(a\) and \(b\) are no longer arbitrary integer coordinates,
2c112bf4 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #so the original lattice count is not preserved automatically. Likewise, logarithms convert
2c112bf4 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #multiplication into addition; they do not directly turn \(a+b\le R\) into \(u\cdot v\le W\) without
2c112bf4 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #an additional construction.
2c112bf5 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c112bf5 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #The GitHub section is standard repository-setup boilerplate and unrelated to the mathematics.
2c112bf5 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
