2c11358f [nitro@ryzen5] this version was for me an introduction to lean and an effort to comprehend prime space
2c1139fe [nitro@ryzen5] the abacus was an attempt to introduce a mechanism to count the rough edges when traverseing the outline of a circle in a gridded space
2c113c6f [kimi-k3.1-pro] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 kimi-k3.1-pro 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.015 10.42KB 38.45s
2c113c6f [kimi-k3.1-pro] #That clarifies the intent: `Basic.lean` is an exploratory construction for learning Lean and
2c113c6f [kimi-k3.1-pro] #investigating prime structure, rather than a completed formalization of Gauss’s circle problem.
2c113c6f [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c113c6f [kimi-k3.1-pro] #For the circle interpretation, the “rough edges” can be made precise as a staircase. In one
2c113c70 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #quadrant, define the column heights
2c113c70 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c113c70 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #\[
2c113c70 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #h_x=\left\lfloor\sqrt{R-x^2}\right\rfloor .
2c113c71 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #\]
2c113c71 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c113c71 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #As \(x\) increases, the changes \(h_{x-1}-h_x\) describe the discrete jumps along the circular
2c113c71 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #outline. Those jumps, together with the boundary columns themselves, determine the lattice count.
2c113c72 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #This also distinguishes two related questions:
2c113c72 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c113c72 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- Local roughness: where and by how much the staircase jumps.
2c113c72 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- Global discrepancy: how the accumulated lattice count differs from the smooth area \(\pi
2c113c73 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #R^2/4\) in a quadrant.
2c113c73 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c113c73 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #Your current `PrimePowerAbacus` instead describes prime-power values \(p^k\) bounded by \(W\). It
2c113c73 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #does not yet record a position, an outline, or a traversal. That is not inherently a problem for an
2c113c73 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #introductory experiment, but the bridge to the circle would need an explicit rule stating what each
2c113c74 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #abacus step counts and how the resulting count relates to the staircase.
2c113c74 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2c113c74 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #A useful next conceptual step would therefore be to choose the invariant first: is the abacus meant
2c113c75 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #to count lattice points, boundary jumps, divisor contributions, or something else? Once that is
2c113c75 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #fixed, the Lean state and constructors can be designed around it, and prime multiplication can be
2c113c75 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #tested as a mechanism rather than assumed to preserve the circle geometry.
2c113c75 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
