2c114531 [gemini-4-argon] #Hello Simon. Looking at the Lean code and the README you've put together, it is clear you spent
2c114531 [gemini-4-argon] #last night exploring two major themes: the mechanics of Dependent Type Theory in Lean and a
2c114531 [gemini-4-argon] #conceptual coordinate transformation from spatial geometry to prime-factor arithmetic.
2c114531 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c114532 [gemini-4-argon] #Here is my reading of your experiments and learnings from the session:
2c114532 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c114532 [gemini-4-argon] #1. Learning Lean: Proofs as Data ("Correctness by Construction")
2c114532 [gemini-4-argon] #In standard programming, you usually define a data structure (like a pair of numbers) and write a
2c114533 [gemini-4-argon] #separate function to validate whether those numbers follow the rules.
2c114533 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c114533 [gemini-4-argon] #Your key Lean learning in `PrimePowerAbacus` was discovering how to bake the rules directly into
2c114533 [gemini-4-argon] #the type definition using inductive types and dependent types:
2c114533 [gemini-4-argon] #• Bounding the Domain: Instead of using raw natural numbers (`ℕ`), you used `Fin (W + 1)`
2c114534 [gemini-4-argon] #to ensure the inputs p, k, q, m are inherently bounded by the width W.
2c114534 [gemini-4-argon] #• Carrying the Proof: Your constructors `step_u` and `step_v` don't just take numbers; they
2c114534 [gemini-4-argon] #demand proofs (`Nat.Prime p.val` and `p.val ^ k.val ≤ W`) before an instance of `PrimePowerAbacus`
2c114535 [gemini-4-argon] #can even exist.
2c114535 [gemini-4-argon] #• Tactic Automation: In your `example` blocks, you learned how to satisfy these proof
2c114535 [gemini-4-argon] #requirements on the fly using tactics like `decide` (for finite decidable propositions) and
2c114535 [gemini-4-argon] #`norm_num` (for arithmetic evaluation), and how to prove a bound cannot be met (\neg (2⁴
2c114536 [gemini-4-argon] #\le 10)).
2c114536 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c114536 [gemini-4-argon] #2. The Mathematical Experiment: From Circles to Prime Space
2c114536 [gemini-4-argon] #Your README outlines an intuitive chain of reductions aimed at taming the "jagged edges" of a
2c114537 [gemini-4-argon] #circle on a grid:
2c114537 [gemini-4-argon] #1. Geometric to Linear: You started with the circle boundary (x² + y² \le R) and imagined
2c114537 [gemini-4-argon] #flattening the squares into a linear boundary (a + b \le R) in a single positive quadrant—turning a
2c114537 [gemini-4-argon] #curve into a uniform staircase (triangular numbers).
2c114538 [gemini-4-argon] #2. Additive to Multiplicative: You then shifted from an additive constraint (a + b \le R)
2c114538 [gemini-4-argon] #to a multiplicative hyperbolic constraint (u · v \le W). In number theory, counting points under
2c114538 [gemini-4-argon] #the hyperbola u · v \le W is the classic Dirichlet Divisor Problem—which is deeply
2c114538 [gemini-4-argon] #analogous to Gauss's Circle Problem! Both problems are famous for having smooth areas with chaotic,
2c114539 [gemini-4-argon] #jagged error terms along their boundaries.
2c114539 [gemini-4-argon] #3. The Abacus Concept: Because u · v \le W is governed by multiplication, the grid points
2c114539 [gemini-4-argon] #are no longer stepped through by +1, but are built out of prime powers (p^k and q^m). You
2c114539 [gemini-4-argon] #conceptualized an "abacus" where the axes (u and v) are traversed via prime-power states rather
2c11453a [gemini-4-argon] #than simple linear increments.
2c11453a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c11453a [gemini-4-argon] #Where the Experiment Paused (The Next Horizon)
2c11453a [gemini-4-argon] #As noted earlier in the thread, your Lean code captures a snapshot of a valid prime power
2c11453b [gemini-4-argon] #on either the u-axis (`step_u`) or the v-axis (`step_v`), rather than a linked chain of
2c11453b [gemini-4-argon] #steps or a combined (u, v) coordinate pair.
2c11453b [gemini-4-argon] #
2c11453b [gemini-4-argon] #Right now, `step_u` and `step_v` replace the `anchor` rather than building on top of a previous
2c11453c [gemini-4-argon] #`PrimePowerAbacus` state, and their bounds are checked independently (p^k \le W and q^m \le W)
2c11453c [gemini-4-argon] #rather than jointly (u · v \le W).
2c11453c [gemini-4-argon] #
2c11453c [gemini-4-argon] #Whenever you're ready to take the next step, we can look at how to make the inductive type actually
2c11453d [gemini-4-argon] #accumulate steps (so it acts like a true traversal or abacus), or explore how the
2c11453d [gemini-4-argon] #hyperbolic divisor grid (u · v \le W) compares to the circular grid (x² + y² \le R). What part of
2c11453d [gemini-4-argon] #last night's experiment stood out most to you?
2c11453d [gemini-4-argon] #
